  "summary": "## Summary: Google Ads Transparency Centre on-demand scraper (free alternative to DataForSEO)\n\n### Changes Implemented\n- Built `scripts/website_analysis/ads_transparency.py`: an on-demand check of Google's own Ads Transparency Centre via its keyless internal RPC (`SearchService/SearchSuggestions` resolves a domain/brand to advertiser IDs; `SearchService/SearchCreatives` lists creatives with format and first/last-shown dates, paginated by cursor). No API key, no login, no $50 deposit.\n- Summary output (`check()`) reuses W4's `web_market.ads_summary` shape exactly (`ads_seen`, `advertising_now` within 30 days, first/last shown, advertisers, verified, formats), so the two sources are directly comparable and storable the same way.\n- Reverse-engineered gotchas built in and tested: SearchCreatives must always send member `\"7\"` (its omission returns `{}`, indistinguishable from \"no ads\"); suggestion counts are integers; region codes are `2000 + ISO numeric` (GB → 2826) and optional, since filtering is disputed; page cursors that repeat stop the run instead of spinning; a 12-request budget caps a run; HTTP 429 / `/sorry/` is recorded as `blocked` and never retried through another route; date-unreadable rows are flagged `dates_read: false` rather than dressed up as a real answer.\n- Politeness + caching follow the web stage's rules: honest user agent, per-host pacing, robots.txt honoured, every raw response cached under `data/raw/ads-transparency-cache/` so re-runs never re-send, plus a `--cache-only` mode.\n- Proxy support (answers the Oxylabs question): `TRANSPARENCY_PROXY_URL`, or `OXYLABS_USERNAME`/`OXYLABS_PASSWORD` (default `pr.oxylabs.io:7777`, host/port overridable). Credentials are read at request time only, used for the request, never cached or logged. Any HTTP(S) IP pool works the same way — Oxylabs Residential/ISP or Web Unblocker,arpa e.g. Bright Data — but plain direct requests should be tried first, since public reports show the RPC answers anonymous, non-fingerprinted calls fine; Google's only defence is a per-IP burst quota that a single polite run is unlikely to reach, so rotation is needed only if 429s become common. Pay-per-GB via Oxylabs is trivially cheaper than the $50 DataForSEO deposit for this volume.\n- CLI (`main()`): `python -m scripts.website_analysis.ads_transparency check --domain X` or `--company 12345678 --db companies-house.db` (domain + name read read-only from the W1 `company_web_identity_current` view), with `--name`, `--regions`, `--max-ads`, `--cache-only`; prints the JSON summary, exits 2 if the run was blocked or errored.\n- 35 new tests (`tests/test_ads_transparency.py`): payload construction, region codes, both date shapes (ISO strings and unix seconds/milliseconds), a parsed row without dates, caching without re-sending, budget/429/`/sorry`/robots/challenge handling, cursor paging and its termination guard, proxy wiring with credentials kept out of the cache, full `check()` flows (match / zero ads / other advertiser / ids fallback / name fallback / blocked / unreadable dates), the SQLite company lookup and both CLI paths from a primed cache.\n- Full suite verified: `927 passed, 1 skipped` (the skip is pre-existing).\n- Documented in `docs/WEB_STAGE.md` (new section \"Free on-demand Ads Transparency check\"), including usage, shape-drift caveats and the proxy env vars.\n\n### Files Modified\n- `scripts/website_analysis/ads_transparency.py` (new)\n- `tests/test_ads_transparency.py` (new)\n- `docs/WEB_STAGE.md` (new section)\n\n### Notes for Developer\n- The payload shapes come from public reverse-engineering write-ups; per repo convention the **first live call of each kind is the check** — run one `check --domain <a domain you know has ads>` once when you're ready, and its cached response should replace the hand-written fixtures in the tests if the live shapes differ from recon. Nothing was sent to Google during implementation (all runs were stubbed or cache-only).\n- The region filter is advisory: one public write-up measured Google ignoring it server-side, so results may include ads shown in other countries.\n- Nothing is wired into the W4 batch stage or stored in SQLite; this is deliberately an on-demand single-company tool. Wiring `check()`'s output into `company_market` later would be mechanical since the `ads` block matches `web_market.ads_summary`.\n- Oxylabs answer in short: yes, it would work (any HTTP(S) proxy is supported through the env vars), but only reach for it if direct runs start getting 429'd regularly — a sticky-session Oxylabs residential pool is the recommended form, and NO workarounds are attempted on a live block (repo policy: blocks are recorded, never routed around)."
}